WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:04.319
These are all the same woman.

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:06.450
Which one do you recognize?

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.450
Why?

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:13.610
How did the Venus de Milo, an armless ancient
statue, go from being lost on a tiny Greek

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.560
island to an international icon?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.500
It’s part of a story that spans centuries
- and a possible cover-up that starts with

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.800
a missing piece right here.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:33.080
This sculpture’s so popular that you can
easily find a 3d scan of it, that’s really

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.790
good quality — and then get it printed.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:42.350
And the sculpture itself provides a clue to
her popularity because it contains her history.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.230
I really hope I don’t screw this up.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:54.040
The story is hidden right here in the seam.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:01:02.440
In 1820, a Greek farmer discovered pieces
of a woman carved from marble on his land.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:08.130
He sold what he found to the French for about
1,000 Francs - today that’d be around $11,000

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.590
US - enough for a decent used car.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:18.940
They took this home - a Venus, split in two,
along with some other scattered marble fragments.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:24.000
Venus has a bunch of imperfections: the arms
were not still attached.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.710
Other flaws marred the body.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:31.170
She was missing her earlobes — probably
taken by looters.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.550
A foot was gone, right here.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.260
And a likely base - called a plinth - had
broken off.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.350
That would have been right here.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:48.360
Each half was meant to be joined in the center
with iron tenons - imagine two metal rods

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.380
joining the two halves.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.560
The rest of the parts likely fell off due
to age.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.550
When she was assembled, her scale was really
impressive,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.630
At 6’7 inches high and with features like
a 12 and a half inch foot.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.100
That’s large.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.820
Shaq-esque foot.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.860
But Venus's looks weren't the most important
factor in her success.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.500
For that you’ve got to look to the circumstances
in which she was dug up, and the French art

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:24.190
world — it was in turmoil.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:29.050
This drawing of the Sphinx was made by Napoleon
Bonaparte’s personal art-looter, Vivant

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.050
Denon.

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:36.000
He drew it during his tour of Napoleon’s
Egypt, while he was searching for art to plunder.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.310
Classical art was considered the most desirable
- a tie to the greatness of Rome and Greece.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.990
While Napoleon spent 20 years trying to take
over the world, Denon took art from everywhere

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.150
Napoleon succeeded.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:54.460
He directed the Louvre museum under Napoleon’s
rule, and filled it with the art that he stole.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:59.450
When Napoleon lost his throne, that massive
cache of classical art had to be returned

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:00.500
to its owners.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.710
Suddenly, the Louvre — and France — had
a huge problem.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:09.620
For example, the Vatican got back Laocoon
and his sons and the Apollo Belvedere, and

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.180
the Italians were reunited with their own
looted Venus, Venus de Midici.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:17.390
The Louvre wasn’t empty, but it was desperate.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.400
Then, five years later, Venus turned up on
an island in Greece.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.480
The French were willing to do anything to
replace that classical art that they lost

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.060
when Napoleon got kicked out.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.590
Even massage the truth.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.940
Remember that plinth, the one that got broken
off the statue?

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:40.910
We know what the plinth looked like thanks
to early drawings.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:46.720
By the sculptor’s name on it and some other
clues, it definitively dated the Venus to

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.800
130-100 BC - what is called the Hellenistic
era.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.050
That was a problem.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:57.620
It was 200 years after the Classical Period
- the one that the critics loved.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.050
So the French tried to hide it.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:04.710
The plinth “got lost” before Venus went
on display at the Louvre in 1821.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:11.340
With the plinth taken care of, the museum’s
director declared Venus was definitely “classical”

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.530
not Hellenistic.

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:18.260
And the Louvre supported critics who called
Venus a work of classical Greek genius.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.160
They gave her a prime spot in the gallery
and insisted it was certainly the well-known

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:26.150
goddess Venus — even though she didn’t
have a label.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.500
Competing interpretations of what Venus’s
missing arms were doing surfaced, and the

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.730
Louvre supported the ones they thought made
Venus look most important.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:37.950
All of this worked.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:44.910
Venus held a prime spot in the galleries and...became
iconic.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:50.030
The Louvre insisted that she was classical,
all the way until 1951, when they finally

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.000
reversed course.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.820
Her unique fame was the result of the Louvre’s
branding campaign to regain national pride.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:06.130
It’s that history that makes her the Venus
we recognize.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:11.610
Maybe the history — is the reason she’s
still worth seeing today.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.830
OK, so one little public service announcement.

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:20.480
If you want to get your own Venus de Milo
3d printed, I put a link to a really nice

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.010
model in the description below.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.450
And I actually got mine done at the local
library.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:30.780
Maybe you can too, and don’t make the same
mistake I did — get yours in hot pink or

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:31.289
lime green.

